# Ethics and Presence
#
# Care ethics as a case dossier: caring theory, skill and moral distress, comfort at the end of life including symptom relief, cultural humility, trauma-informed care, and the court cases behind advance directives.

#>nursing_ethics
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!nursing
#:form #!dossier
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Ethics and Presence"
 ~#claim: "Nursing ethics joins presence with action: at the end of life, comfort includes relief of breathlessness and pain, and patients and their surrogates may refuse treatment they do not want."
 ~#proof: "End-of-life care from the 2008 ACCM consensus and Sykes and Thorns 2003; refusal of treatment from Quinlan 1976, Cruzan 1990, and the Patient Self-Determination Act; cultural humility from Tervalon and Murray-Garcia 1998."
 ~#door: "Read the three court cases, then comfort care, then the frameworks for skill and distress."
 ~#audience: "nursing students, families, and readers facing decisions about care"
 continuity: "Ethics and Presence | comfort includes relief"
 cold_open: "At the end of life, comfort includes relief from pain and breathlessness, and presence means staying to give it."
}

